A 120° rotation about the center of ABC preserves the uniform distribution of P and cycles the sides AB, BC, and CA. Thus each side is equally likely to be the base of the unique largest subtriangle.

To rule out ties with positive probability, note that the bases have equal length, so two areas are equal exactly when P has equal perpendicular distances to their base lines. Inside ABC, such points lie on the angle bisector at the shared vertex. The three bisectors have zero area, so almost surely there is a unique largest subtriangle. The three equally likely possibilities therefore each have probability 1/3.
Final answer: \frac{1}{3}
